        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-24c0f2a24dedefd8\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Arrigo’s “Ti amo” primarily avows his loving state and answers Loretta’s question about why he has kissed her. The embrace and subsequent possessive concern give slight support to its also invoking their romantic bond, but the words do not clearly undertake a commitment. Despite the intensely sensual setting, nothing presents the utterance as a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited ambiguity over P: the declaration may help enact or affirm the lovers’ relationship, but no definite promise, obligation, or relational undertaking is expressed.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Arrigo directly says “Ti amo” to Loretta in reply to her asking what he is doing after he kisses her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration functions as an explanation and avowal within an already reciprocal, highly sensual encounter. Loretta answers through an explicitly loving embrace, and the narration describes both as enamored. Arrigo’s later demand, “Sei mia,” and his question whether she truly loves him reveal possessiveness and insecurity, but those contextual features do not turn the core declaration into a clear commitment or an expressive reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“— Che fai?... — diss’ella … — Ti amo, — egli rispose” (“What are you doing? … ‘I love you,’ he replied”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit response structure strongly supports T: Arrigo presents love as the reason for his kiss.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He says it while encircling her with his arms and looking into her eyes; she responds with “un movimento pieno d’amore” and embraces him blissfully.\",\"supports\":\"The embodied romantic setting corroborates a sincere-seeming love avowal, while by itself not establishing E or a commitment-level P.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration describes “il palpito delle loro anime innamorate” (“the beating of their enamored souls”).\",\"supports\":\"Narrative framing reinforces the interpretation of the utterance as presenting an actual loving state.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Arrigo later asks, “Volevo sapere se mi ami, se mi ami davvero...” (“I wanted to know whether you love me, whether you truly love me”).\",\"supports\":\"His concern with reciprocal love supports the avowal reading and gives weak relational-force support, but supplies no explicit undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit “I love you” form and retained as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the selected core expression is a literal first-person present love declaration.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the principal avowal, with slight P support for relational invocation. E is unnecessary because sensual intensity and immediate delivery do not independently show reflex-like expressive force, and no residual core function requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
